      "writtenOn": "2026-10-05T16:29:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Only the source image is checked for !isTextureBacked(); the layer device isn\u0027t. internalDrawDeviceWithFilter runs for Ganesh/Graphite devices too, so with a raster-backed SkImage drawn into a GPU layer the cached skif::FilterResult holds a context-bound texture. That result then sits in a process-global table shared across contexts and threads: a hit from another GrDirectContext draws a foreign texture, and LRU eviction under the mutex destroys GPU resources on whatever thread happens to insert. Needs a device-side guard (CPU raster only) or no caching for GPU layers.",
      "revId": "d1831165aff28c8dbc7e6bccbf319869edb17863",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9fa2aa79_7bd26dba",
        "filename": "src/core/SkDraw.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1016,
      "author": {
        "id": 3661524
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-05T16:29:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "SkPath::getGenerationID() returns uint64_t on main (SkPathData::uniqueID, 64-bit atomic counter). Storing it in a uint32_t silently truncates, so two distinct live paths collide once the counter passes 2^32 within a process, and the cache then returns the wrong device path. A long-lived GPU process doing OOP raster allocates new SkPaths per frame per deserialized op, so this is reachable. Same truncation in devPathGenID (SkDraw.h), BlurredPathMaskKey::fDevPathGenID and AAClipPathKey::fPathGenID.\n\nSeparately: Skia\u0027s existing genID-keyed caches register SkIDChangeListeners so entries die with the path; these caches don\u0027t, which is why the collision matters.",
      "revId": "d1831165aff28c8dbc7e6bccbf319869edb17863",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
